# Review ownership — SAD §16.1.
#
# "CODEOWNERS review required for `packages/split-engine`, `packages/domain`,
# migrations and CI config."
#
# WHY THESE PATHS AND NOT "everything": a CODEOWNERS file that claims the whole
# repository is never actually enforced, because every PR trivially satisfies it
# and reviewers learn to ignore the request. These four are the paths where a
# quiet mistake is expensive — money arithmetic, the layer every other layer
# depends on, the schema history, and the pipeline itself.
#
# A real team name replaces the placeholder below. Until then GitHub silently
# ignores an owner that does not exist, which is a failure mode worth knowing
# about rather than discovering during an incident.

# The financial core: split arithmetic and the domain layer both carry the 100%
# coverage gates from SAD §15.2, and a rounding change here is a money bug.
/packages/split-engine/        @ses/core-team
/packages/domain/              @ses/core-team

# Schema history. A migration that has already been applied cannot be edited, only
# superseded, so review has to happen before merge.
/supabase/migrations/          @ses/core-team
/apps/api/src/infrastructure/database/migrations/  @ses/core-team
/apps/api/drizzle.config.ts    @ses/core-team

# CI and the quality gates it enforces. Without this, a PR that weakens a gate can
# approve itself.
/.github/                      @ses/core-team
/.dependency-cruiser.js        @ses/core-team
/eslint.config.js              @ses/core-team
/turbo.json                    @ses/core-team
/package.json                  @ses/core-team
/pnpm-lock.yaml                @ses/core-team

# The API contract. A breaking change here reaches every shipped mobile build.
/packages/contracts/           @ses/core-team @ses/mobile-team
/docs/api/OPENAPI.yaml         @ses/core-team @ses/mobile-team
